The problem is a Rounding Mode Issue.
Your input value likely has decimals (e.g., 1204658.90 or 1204658.55).
 * Current Behavior (HALF_UP): The system is rounding the number to the nearest neighbor.
   * -1204658.55 becomes -1204659.00 (Rounds Away from zero).
 * Expected Behavior (DOWN/Truncate): You want to simply chop off the decimals without rounding.
   * -1204658.55 becomes -1204658.00.
✅ The Fix
I will add a DOWN logic option to the RuleParser. This allows you to "Truncate" the value before formatting it.
1. Update RuleParser.java
Update the applyDecimalLogic method to support "down" (Truncate).
    // In RuleParser.java

    public static BigDecimal applyDecimalLogic(BigDecimal amount, String head, int colIdx, String decimalConfig) {
        if (decimalConfig == null) return amount;
        
        Matcher m = RULE_PATTERN.matcher(decimalConfig);
        while (m.find()) {
            String headRegex = m.group(1);
            String colReq = m.group(2);
            String rule = m.group(3);

            boolean colMatch = colReq.isEmpty() || colReq.equals(String.valueOf(colIdx));
            boolean headMatch = Pattern.compile(headRegex).matcher(head).lookingAt();

            if (colMatch && headMatch) {
                if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("floor")) {
                    amount = amount.setScale(0, RoundingMode.FLOOR);
                } else if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("ceil")) {
                    amount = amount.setScale(0, RoundingMode.CEILING);
                } else if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("round")) {
                    amount = amount.setScale(0, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
                
                // --- NEW CODE START ---
                } else if (rule.equalsIgnoreCase("down")) {
                    // Truncates (Chops off) decimals: -120.99 -> -120
                    amount = amount.setScale(0, RoundingMode.DOWN); 
                // --- NEW CODE END ---
                
                } else {
                    try {
                        int scale = Integer.parseInt(rule);
                        amount = amount.setScale(scale, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
                    } catch (NumberFormatException e) {}
                }
            }
        }
        return amount;
    }

2. Update Database Config
You need to apply this new down rule before applying the decimal formatting.
Logic: "Truncate decimals first, THEN format to 2 decimal places."
UPDATE ASCII_CONFIG SET 
    -- Add [down] before [2]
    OUTPUT_AMT_DECIMAL = '["^.*"][5][down]|["^.*"][1][down]|["^.*"][5][2]|["^.*"][1][2]'
WHERE ID = 103;

COMMIT;

